.TH lrat-auth-login 1  lrat 
.SH NAME
lrat\-auth\-login \- Check an API key with Linear and store it in the OS keyring
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlrat auth login\fR [\fB\-\-stdin\fR] [\fB\-\-from\-keyring\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Check an API key with Linear and store it in the OS keyring
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
Read the key from standard input instead of prompting for it
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-keyring\fR [\fI<SERVICE/ACCOUNT>\fR]
Read the key from an existing OS keyring entry instead of prompting for it

The entry is named SERVICE/ACCOUNT and defaults to linear/api\-key. On Linux it is a Secret Service item (GNOME Keyring, KWallet) with the attributes `service` and `account`, as `secret\-tool store` writes it, or `service` and `username`; on macOS it is a generic password in the Keychain with that service and account. lrat checks the key with Linear, then stores a copy under its own entry; the entry read is left as it is. For another entry, name it: `\-\-from\-keyring work/linear\-key`.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
